{#
    `.. contents::` — what is on this page.

    Addressed rather than written. The element marks the section the reader has
    scrolled to, which is the one thing about this list no renderer knows, and
    a `nav.contents` of our own would be the same list with nothing following
    the reader. It is handed the sections and owns all of its markup — the
    `<nav>` it is, the label over it, and every entry.

    `sds-aside` is the column it stands in where the page has the room for one.
    It spans the document, so the list can rest anywhere in it; the element is
    what rests, and is a second element for that reason. Narrower, the box is
    not there at all and the list is a block where the author wrote it.
#}
{%- set label = node.caption ? renderNode(node.caption)|striptags|trim : 'On this page' -%}
<div class="sds-aside">
    <sds-nav-toc label="{{ label }}" entries="{{ sections(node)|json_encode }}"></sds-nav-toc>
</div>
